m operates using the \\\"10\\\" decimal. This longlasting convention still works well and has deeply ingrained in our computation. Yet we know as well that computers are using binary system, thus the things written in decimal can be translated into binary for computers to read. Why does computer adopt binary system instead of decimal? If we continue our convention here, then wouldn't it be more convenient and efficient and that we no longer needs programing? The answer is simple (despite that I feel that we're branching off from our original topic and stepping into computer science, but I'd like to resolve your concern here): computers contain sophisticated circuits and the states of circuits can only have two different possibilities: 1. a current goes through that circuit; 2. no current goes through that current. Therefore, \\\"1\\\" and \\\"0\\\" well fit into this scenario, rendering binary system to be the primary system contemporary computers rely on.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThen, we'd like to also figure out how metrics of the same physical quantities are converted between one and another. Normally, the two different metrics follow a constant-ratio relationship. For example, \\u003cspan class=\\\"katex\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-mathml\\\"\\u003e\\u003cmath xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\\\"\\u003e\\u003csemantics\\u003e\\u003cmrow\\u003e\\u003cmn\\u003e1\\u003c/mn\\u003e\\u003c/mrow\\u003e\\u003cannotation encoding=\\\"application/x-tex\\\"\\u003e\\\\mathrm1\\u003c/annotation\\u003e\\u003c/semantics\\u003e\\u003c/math\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-html\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-base\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-strut\\\" style=\\\"height:0.6444em\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathrm\\\"\\u003e1\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e mile is roughly equal to \\u003cspan class=\\\"katex\\\"\\u003e\