more complicated ones or not, but as long as you know the relationship between one metric and another, the conversion will work out accordingly. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd some may ask that why is the relationship between Fahrenheit degree and Celsius degree so weird. I'd like to end this section with the answer to this question. It's highly relevant to measurement: the nature or definition of measurement is indeed comparing relatively using a reference. We cannot directly know the height of the classroom door or the weight of your cat by looking at them. We need to compare it to some more uniformly defined thing, which is \\\"reference\\\". So, a short answer to this question is that the references used by the two temperature unit system are different: Celsius degree system sets zero celsius degree as the temperature liquid water begins to freeze (or in reverse, solid water (ice) begins to melt into liquid water), while setting \\u003cspan class=\\\"katex\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-mathml\\\"\\u003e\\u003cmath xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\\\"\\u003e\\u003csemantics\\u003e\\u003cmrow\\u003e\\u003cmn\\u003e100\\u003c/mn\\u003e\\u003c/mrow\\u003e\\u003cannotation encoding=\\\"application/x-tex\\\"\\u003e\\\\mathrm100\\u003c/annotation\\u003e\\u003c/semantics\\u003e\\u003c/math\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-html\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-base\\\"\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"katex-strut\\\" style=\\\"height:0.6444em\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord mathrm\\\"\\u003e1\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003cspan class=\\\"mord\\\"\\u003e00\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e\\u003c/span\\u003e celsius degree as the temperature at which liquid water begins to boil into gaseous water (the Modern SI definition of Celsius temperature is tightly tied to Kelvin scale). Notice that these two temperatures are set under the standard atmosphere pressure, s